      "content": "Here is a highly optimized, max-score Cheat Sheet for **SkillStream Academy** on **Introduction to Python Loops**:\n\n**Understanding Iteration in Python Loops**\nEvery **for loop** and **while loop** fundamentally relies on the concept of **iteration** to process data. When you call the **range** function, it generates a numeric **sequence** that drives the **iteration** of your program's logic. You can also manually set up a counter variable to handle **increment** operations and often need to **decrement** it to meet stopping criteria.\n\n**The For Loop and Range Mechanics**\nA standard **for loop** automatically handles the **iterator** for you, making it ideal for **sequence** objects like lists or strings. If you need more control, you can switch to a **while loop** where you explicitly manage the **increment** or **decrement** of your loop variable. Using a **range** object ensures you never accidentally create an **infinite loop** by forgetting to check your termination condition.\n\n**Loop Control and Safety**\nEffective **loop control** is vital to prevent your script from running indefinitely. You should always include a **break** statement to exit a **for loop**",
